[{:db/id "q-93985/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" :answer/id #uuid "0f8667dd-b3a9-52c2-8135-496dca8c2f64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0" :answer/id #uuid "6d2c524d-75c8-509f-8e3a-4dc728edd52e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" :answer/id #uuid "958014bb-2921-5227-a9a3-13c2c2c9d303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" :answer/id #uuid "dfc9aecc-eb6d-5498-9bbe-69d0e051fe7a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c" :answer/id #uuid "fa984d91-9c17-5957-ab68-40dbb92a0d89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "559ed0ee-30e4-55d6-a67b-7f25a3481dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-93985/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-93985/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" "q-93985/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0" "q-93985/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" "q-93985/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" "q-93985/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-93985" :question/id #uuid "f3b547ce-eddc-5153-a8ef-15edfde23fe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93985/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-93985"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-93583"] :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection/3440ffb430bf09b9cc3be28b3ac0b5af1ddc80affb14810b6e5c974edac79ba7" :answer/id #uuid "1abd424b-ab9d-567a-aa9a-392f2bf8a6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection/221ad037b15a166b5ed8c3d95c75282b0d811c56b50fdfe8ce629e5c87034a8e" :answer/id #uuid "eedf2233-cd35-51dd-ba8a-acdfb24fada6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" :answer/id #uuid "a11ef24c-e275-5da0-b9cc-7a5868387040" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection/2e147b9bf74fe5a9fe1cc06d49c56b8c05578bddb93b65358c386cfaa2e4aa36" :answer/id #uuid "b4dbaa75-a5a2-5947-9bbd-cbd505eedb99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0" :answer/id #uuid "e02c8b56-9c90-5ce6-98c1-e9cb055d4d29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5528bf15-c00b-5418-b305-184e01d61a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140504/selection/3440ffb430bf09b9cc3be28b3ac0b5af1ddc80affb14810b6e5c974edac79ba7" :answer-field/choices ["q-140504/selection/3440ffb430bf09b9cc3be28b3ac0b5af1ddc80affb14810b6e5c974edac79ba7" "q-140504/selection/221ad037b15a166b5ed8c3d95c75282b0d811c56b50fdfe8ce629e5c87034a8e" "q-140504/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" "q-140504/selection/2e147b9bf74fe5a9fe1cc06d49c56b8c05578bddb93b65358c386cfaa2e4aa36" "q-140504/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140504" :question/id #uuid "d998fa5b-63b3-55ca-a8aa-c2f7bc2f2a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140504/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140504"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" :answer/id #uuid "d930335b-0e39-59bc-a451-12eb58f6206e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" :answer/id #uuid "fd4003ac-da2d-5dc2-8be8-2c6e8deeef4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0" :answer/id #uuid "267f83ee-a054-5845-b9de-d36cda768259" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" :answer/id #uuid "5824191e-ed48-5107-b882-fd6c45280102" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c" :answer/id #uuid "a83873b8-2132-54f4-8d14-e585901b42d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6859063-5785-5bc0-af31-6858d48a5f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-93993/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-93993/selection/f6ed8c3e8ac11c4c476e856e67261d9addd41c460d2f0404e6d3f3a72ad0633a" "q-93993/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" "q-93993/selection/5f6c142a82c4a0bf971b412708f6830f8e50a71860d9c4cebbd361a473710cf0" "q-93993/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" "q-93993/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-93993" :question/id #uuid "2af9cef0-49a6-59d3-80eb-25677266088b" :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93993/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-93993"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c" :answer/id #uuid "2ee6595b-4a0e-553c-8249-72f4b6b609b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection/2e147b9bf74fe5a9fe1cc06d49c56b8c05578bddb93b65358c386cfaa2e4aa36" :answer/id #uuid "43ebfd72-668a-55f2-ae3e-c80c0a87e537" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" :answer/id #uuid "13755144-f63e-5350-80a1-c1a769e70153" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" :answer/id #uuid "f1eaa499-034f-5dec-b795-eabdd59655b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection/221ad037b15a166b5ed8c3d95c75282b0d811c56b50fdfe8ce629e5c87034a8e" :answer/id #uuid "c076c3e4-ca30-53e1-9053-0585f557ff4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a310c5ec-c97a-55cb-ad91-cc33ccba7dea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143679/selection/221ad037b15a166b5ed8c3d95c75282b0d811c56b50fdfe8ce629e5c87034a8e" :answer-field/choices ["q-143679/selection/820282211c30a20e021a66f68b7464203ed7fe571ec86a81ae83e0d42d754f2c" "q-143679/selection/2e147b9bf74fe5a9fe1cc06d49c56b8c05578bddb93b65358c386cfaa2e4aa36" "q-143679/selection/afe4b97c823938f24ee0f462e6dae1ce1a6e74074abfb6f3f9af3b253499f0cc" "q-143679/selection/be5beffd9a95134974e6bda496907ef99198947107984fe7477f4a7dcf5134a9" "q-143679/selection/221ad037b15a166b5ed8c3d95c75282b0d811c56b50fdfe8ce629e5c87034a8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143679" :question/id #uuid "14c9b2e9-b22a-597e-94a1-26642435278d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143679" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-143679/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143679"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96046"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "457390a0-5131-5259-acb4-ecfeccf19c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8adbf214-32e8-55c6-a088-88fb93e6f038" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "493aa98f-b668-58dd-931c-ca7dc32fa045" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "98227d7d-cca3-5c23-a956-ad1eec43a74a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3c5860e9-2de7-5b1f-9199-643c31716303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7455d6-80b1-5c51-8e56-df2b42a9e44d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96592/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-96592/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96592/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96592/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96592/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-96592/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96592" :question/id #uuid "3ce569a7-84a0-586c-9965-dabc3caf2d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-96592" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-96592/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96592"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96596/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2bc01e52-6557-5e4e-